## Section 1: Motivation and Empirical Landscape

### 1.1 The Governance Gap

The deployment of robotic systems accelerates across heterogeneous governance domains. Industrial robotics operates within established safety frameworks, yet emerging capability areas develop under fragmented national regimes. Military robotics presents the most acute governance gap.

Three critical temporal anchors characterize the institutional landscape. The Group of Governmental Experts on Lethal Autonomous Weapons Systems adopted a report in September 2026 containing elements of an instrument without prejudging legal nature. The Seventh CCW Review Conference in November 2026 will determine institutional trajectory. ISO/TC 299 continues active work on service-robot modularity and humanoid safety standards. The EU AI Omnibus shifts high-risk AI embedded in machinery toward the Machinery Regulation via delegated acts due by August 2028.

Three structural problems emerge from this heterogeneous landscape. Regulatory fragmentation creates multiplicative compliance costs and arbitrage incentives. Temporal mismatch between institutional adaptation and technological change creates persistent oversight gaps. Asymmetric capacity across states enables regulatory dumping dynamics.

### 1.2 Three Distinguishing Features of Robotics

Physicality and irreversibility distinguish robotics from software-only AI systems. Robots act in the physical world with irreversible consequences, rendering trial-and-error deployment approaches unavailable.

Dual-use embeddedness characterizes virtually every robotic capability. Navigation, manipulation, perception, and autonomy are domain-agnostic, making export controls simultaneously more important and more difficult than in nuclear or software cases.

Decentralized production fundamentally limits choke-point governance models. Robotic systems do not require rare materials, massive capital investment, or concentrated expertise.

### 1.3 Historical Analog Calibration

To anchor theoretical parameters without conducting physical experiments, we draw on three institutional precedents.

Nuclear verification through IAEA safeguards provides baseline for hardware attestation capacity. Detection probability for diversion of significant quantities of fissile material approaches 0.95 under comprehensive safeguards, requiring substantial infrastructure investment. We calibrate hardware attestation effectiveness in the range 0.4 to 0.6 for robotics.

Chemical Weapons Convention challenge inspection demonstrates feasibility of dual-use facility verification but with political constraints. Detection probability for undeclared production is estimated between 0.3 and 0.5 depending on facility type. We calibrate software transparency effectiveness between 0.1 and 0.2 for software-defined systems, reflecting greater difficulty of verifying code behavior compared to physical facilities.

Montreal Protocol success derived from clear technical alternatives, trade restrictions on non-parties, and multilateral capacity building. We calibrate diplomatic friction reduction through technical delegation at approximately forty percent of baseline when pre-delegation is high.

### 1.4 Path-Dependent Feasible Space

The November 2026 CCW Review Conference outcome constrains the feasible parameter space for verification architectures.

**Definition 1.1 (Path-Dependent Parameter Space).** Let \(\Omega(t) \subset \mathbb{R}^K\) denote feasible governance parameters at time \(t\). The CCW outcome \(\omega \in \{\omega_{\text{neg}}, \omega_{\text{disc}}, \omega_{\text{stall}}\}\) determines:

\[
\Omega(\omega) = \Omega_{\text{base}} \cap \Omega_{\text{CCW}}(\omega)
\]

where \(\Omega_{\text{base}}\) represents technological and economic constraints, and \(\Omega_{\text{CCW}}(\omega)\) represents diplomatic and political constraints.

**Proposition 1.1 (Path-Dependent Feasibility).** The feasible coalition window is non-empty only if:

\[
\eta(\omega) \leq \frac{\tau_{\text{crit}} - \tau_0}{\left( \frac{m_{\min}(\omega) - 1}{n - 1} \right)^\alpha}
\]

This condition requires diplomatic friction to remain sufficiently small relative to the gap between critical delay and baseline latency, given the minimum deterrence threshold.

