## Section 7: Conclusion

This thesis develops a formal, three-layer theory of global robotic governance institutions, integrating game-theoretic, control-theoretic, and mechanism design approaches into a unified framework. The central contributions include the Feasible Coalition Window with explicit functional forms and numerical examples, the Non-Refundable Sink Principle solving the Holmström impossibility under sovereignty constraints, two-level ratification integration capturing domestic political constraints, non-contiguous coalition modeling with coordination costs, calibrated parameter ranges anchored to institutional analogs, and path-dependent scenarios for post-November 2026 CCW outcomes.

The framework is explicitly theoretical. No physical experiments or field tests have been conducted. The results are derived from established mathematical frameworks with parameters anchored to historical institutional analogs.

The central insight: global governance of robotics is not always feasible. Its existence depends on verification technology, diplomatic friction, enforcement credibility, and path-dependent institutional outcomes. Where feasible, the proposed architecture—pre-delegated technical authority, non-refundable sink enforcement, nested coalitions—maximizes the probability of stable governance within the narrow feasible corridor.

The governance of robotics is a problem of institutional engineering under strategic, dynamic, and informational constraints. Formal analysis reveals both structural limits and design levers for navigating these constraints successfully. This thesis demonstrates that such problems are analytically tractable and that formal analysis can inform institutional design in ways that purely qualitative approaches cannot.

