### E.2 Auxiliary Lemma — Monotonicity of Delay

**Lemma E.1.** \(\tau(m)\) is strictly increasing in \(m\) and in \(\eta_{\text{eff}}\).

**Proof.**  
For \(m_2 > m_1\):

\[
\tau(m_2) - \tau(m_1) = \eta \left[ \left( \frac{m_2 - 1}{n - 1} \right)^\alpha - \left( \frac{m_1 - 1}{n - 1} \right)^\alpha \right] > 0
\]

since \(\alpha > 0\) and \(\eta > 0\). Similarly, \(\partial \tau / \partial \eta > 0\) for \(m > 1\). \(\blacksquare\)

**Corollary E.1.1.** Any institutional design that lowers \(\eta_{\text{eff}}\) (pre-delegation to TSA, reduction of bilateral frictions) strictly expands the upper bound of \(\mathcal{W}\).

### E.3 Auxiliary Lemma — Coalition Contiguity under Coordination Costs

**Lemma E.2.** Stable coalitions need not be contiguous in technical capacity when coordination costs are non-uniform.

**Proof.**  
Consider two high-capacity states \(i, j\) with \(\gamma_{ij}\) prohibitive. The marginal benefit of including both may be outweighed by the coordination cost, while including a lower-capacity state \(k\) with \(\gamma_{ik} \approx \gamma_{jk} \approx 0\) may be beneficial. The internal stability condition:

\[
U_i(S) \geq U_i(S \setminus \{i\})
\]

depends on both capacity and coordination costs. Non-contiguity follows from the possibility that coordination savings exceed capacity deficits. \(\blacksquare\)

